<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caratacus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Caractacus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caratacus was a British chieftain, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a> of the Catuvellauni, who led resistance to the Roman invasion of 43 CE. After the fall of his homeland he continued the war among the Silures and Ordovices of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, until he was defeated around 50. He fled to the Brigantes, whose queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartimandua">Cartimandua</a> handed him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> spared his life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caratacus.jpg" alt="Caratacus" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CaractacusClaudius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Caratacus</b> led the Silures and Ordovices in Wales until his defeat in 51, after which Cartimandua, queen of the Brigantes, handed him over to the Romans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Cartimandua handed the fugitive <b>Caratacus</b> to the Romans in 51, and later her divorce from her consort Venutius split the Brigantes into rival factions.</blockquote><a href="/state/brigantes">Brigantes</a></li><li><blockquote>After Cunobelinus died, his sons Togodumnus and <b>Caratacus</b> provoked Rome by attacking the Atrebates (Britain).</blockquote><a href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a></li><li><blockquote>They enter written history through Tacitus, who records that the British leader <b>Caratacus</b> moved his war against Rome into their territory around 51 after leaving the Silures.</blockquote><a href="/state/ordovices">Ordovices</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Caratacus</b> fought alongside them after 47, and in 52 they defeated a legion under Manlius Valens; the governor Ostorius Scapula died worn out by the campaign.</blockquote><a href="/state/silures">Silures</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=64" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Caratacus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=64">What was here in 64 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I had men and horses, arms and wealth. What wonder if I parted with them reluctantly? If you Romans choose to lord it over the world, does it follow that the world is to accept slavery?&#8221;</p><footer>Speech before Claudius in Rome, 51 CE (Tacitus, Annals, book 12) &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1876 &#183; Tacitus' account &#183; <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Tacitus/annals.mb.txt" rel="noopener">The Internet Classics Archive</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My punishment would be followed by oblivion, whereas, if you save my life, I shall be an everlasting memorial of your clemency.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech before Claudius in Rome, 51 CE (Tacitus, Annals, book 12) &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1876 &#183; Tacitus' account &#183; <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Tacitus/annals.mb.txt" rel="noopener">The Internet Classics Archive</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
